🧠 1. Basic if + remove (most explicit)
==========
if (window.get(key) == 0) {
    window.remove(key);
}
============

🚀 2. computeIfPresent (clean + atomic style)
===
window.computeIfPresent(key, (k, v) -> v == 1 ? null : v - 1);
===
👉 Returning null from the lambda = remove the key
This is perfect for sliding window frequency maps.

🧩 3. compute (works for both present + absent)
=======
window.compute(key, (k, v) -> {
    if (v == null) return null;
    int newVal = v - 1;
    return newVal == 0 ? null : newVal;
});
=========
Use this when key might or might not exist.

🧹 4. remove(key, value) (conditional remove)
========
window.remove(key, 0);
=========
👉 removes only if key maps to that exact value
Nice and concise.

🧨 6. entrySet().removeIf(...) (bulk removal)
========
window.entrySet().removeIf(e -> e.getValue() == 0);
=========
👍 Pros

Removes multiple entries at once

Good for cleanup pass

❌ 7. values().removeIf(...)
===
window.values().removeIf(v -> v == 0);
====

This removes entries by value, but:

you don’t control which key


🧵 8. Iterator-based removal (safe during iteration)
===
Iterator<Map.Entry<Integer,Integer>> it = window.entrySet().iterator();

while (it.hasNext()) {
    Map.Entry<Integer,Integer> e = it.next();
    if (e.getValue() == 0) {
        it.remove();  // safe removal
    }
}
===